Carafe: the profiler splits FEX's time outside translated blocks into block entries and dispatcher parts.
Under FEX a sample whose PC lies in FEX's code but not in the block the state names went to "jit". Two
cases make that time: the first two instructions of every block, adr x0 to the block's header and the
store of x0 to the state, run before the state names the new block; and FEX's dispatcher, a 16 KB
buffer with the L1-miss lookup, the linker and compile path, the 64-bit divide helpers, the x87
sin/cos/tan/atan/log/exp handlers and the softfloat call thunks (Dispatcher.cpp:61-600). A sample on a
block's first two instructions is now resolved through the adr to its block and counted as x86; the rest
is placed by the dispatcher pointers in the thread's CpuStateFrame, and a new line gives the shares.
Only memory reads, so the paused thread holds nothing up.
LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the file it changes.

